const canonical = "https://samedayappliance.repair/services/dryer-repair/speed-queen-not-heating/";

const title = "Speed Queen Dryer Not Heating? Causes & Fix — Same Day";
const description = "Speed Queen dryer runs but won't heat? Its commercial-grade design is simple — usually a clogged vent (a fire risk) then the heating element, thermal limit, or gas igniter. Safe checks and when to call.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my Speed Queen dryer not heating?",
    a: "Speed Queen dryers are built commercial-grade and simple, so a no-heat call comes down to a short list of rugged parts. The leading cause is a restricted exhaust vent that makes the dryer overheat and trips a thermal limit or the thermal fuse. After the vent, an electric Speed Queen's heating element can burn open; a gas model's burner may not light because of a weak igniter, failed gas-valve coils, or a bad flame sensor. Clean the vent first — a thermal limit that tripped on a blocked vent will just trip again until the airflow is fixed."
  },
  {
    q: "Does a Speed Queen dryer show error codes?",
    a: "On most models, no — and that's by design. Speed Queen builds to a commercial standard with fewer electronics than a typical consumer dryer, so a no-heat unit usually just runs cold with nothing on the display. You diagnose it by testing the heating parts rather than reading a code. That mechanical simplicity is a big part of why Speed Queens are known for lasting: fewer control boards and sensors means fewer things to fail, and the parts that do wear are straightforward, rugged components. If your Speed Queen won't heat, the fix is almost always a single hard part, not a mysterious control fault."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I fix a Speed Queen dryer that won't heat myself?",
    a: "The safe, high-value step is yours: clean the lint filter and clear the entire exhaust vent — the hose behind the dryer and the exterior flap included. That resolves most no-heat calls and removes a real fire hazard. Also reset both halves of the 240V breaker on an electric Speed Queen; if one leg trips, the drum tumbles but the heat is dead. What you shouldn't do is open the cabinet to test the heating element or thermal limit, and never touch the gas line or valve on a gas model. Those are tech jobs."
  },
  {
    q: "Is a dryer that won't heat a fire hazard?",
    a: "Yes — the clogged vent that most often stops a Speed Queen from heating is also a leading fire cause. The U.S. Fire Administration reports roughly 15,600 dryer fires a year, and the NFPA finds failure to clean the dryer and its venting is the number-one factor, with lint the most common material to ignite. A Speed Queen running hot or refusing to heat is worth clearing the same day — it fixes the heat and removes the hazard together. Even a rugged commercial-grade dryer depends on a clear vent."
  },
  {
    q: "Is it worth repairing a Speed Queen dryer that won't heat?",
    a: "Almost always. Speed Queens are built to last, and the no-heat parts — heating element, thermal limit, and thermal fuse on an electric model, or the igniter, gas-valve coils, and flame sensor on a gas model — are rugged, straightforward, and among the more affordable dryer repairs. Given how long these machines run, fixing a no-heat Speed Queen is usually the easy call over replacing it. Our techs verify the vent first, then test the heating circuit and replace the failed part. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair."
  }
];

