<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="dryer-repair/speed-queen-not-heating"
  eyebrow="Speed Queen Dryer Not Heating · Diagnostic Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="Speed Queen Dryer Runs but Won't Heat? Check the Vent First"
  subtitle="Speed Queen's commercial-grade build is simple and rugged, so a no-heat call is usually a clogged vent — a fire risk — then a heating element or gas igniter. Here's what to check and when it's a tech job."
  heroImageTitle="Speed Queen Dryer Not Heating Repair"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A Speed Queen that won't heat is usually a choked vent and a tripped limit.</h2>

    <p><strong>Speed Queen dryers are built commercial-grade and simple, so a no-heat call comes down to a short list of rugged parts. The leading cause is a restricted exhaust vent that overheats the dryer and trips a thermal limit or the thermal fuse. After the vent, an electric Speed Queen's heating element can burn open; a gas model's burner may not light because of a weak igniter, failed gas-valve coils, or a bad flame sensor. Most Speed Queens show no error code — by design, they run fewer electronics — so you diagnose by testing the heating parts. Clean the filter and full vent first; a limit that tripped on a blocked vent will just trip again until the airflow is fixed. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Why the vent comes first — and fast:</strong> the lint-clogged vent that stops your Speed Queen from heating is a leading cause of house fires. The U.S. Fire Administration counts about 15,600 dryer fires a year, and the NFPA finds failure to clean the dryer and its venting is the number-one factor. Even a rugged commercial-grade dryer depends on a clear vent — clear it the same day.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 SIMPLICITY (differentiator) -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · Built simple on purpose</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Fewer electronics means a simpler no-heat fix.</h2>

    <p>Speed Queen builds its dryers to a commercial standard — heavier cabinets, robust components, and deliberately fewer electronics than a typical consumer dryer. That's a big part of why they're known for lasting, and it changes what a no-heat repair looks like: with few control boards or sensors, a Speed Queen that won't heat almost always has a single failed hard part (element, thermal limit, fuse, or a gas burner component) rather than a murky control fault to chase.</p>

    <p>It also means there's usually no diagnostic code to read — that's normal, not a sign the machine is dead. Our techs test the heating circuit directly, which on a Speed Queen is straightforward, and the rugged parts are affordable. For the general picture across brands, see the <a href="/services/dryer-repair/not-heating/">dryer not heating</a> guide.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a Speed Queen dryer stops heating</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs find them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Restricted vent → tripped thermal limit / fuse (most common)</h3>
      <p>A lint-clogged filter or duct chokes airflow, the dryer overheats, and a thermal limit or the one-time thermal fuse trips and cuts the heat. It only stays fixed once the vent that tripped it is cleared.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Heating element (electric models)</h3>
      <p>The element heats the air on an electric Speed Queen. When it burns open, the dryer tumbles cold with no code. Tested for continuity and replaced inside the cabinet — a tech job, but a rugged, straightforward part.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Thermal limit / thermostat</h3>
      <p>Speed Queen uses thermostats and a high-limit to regulate and cap the temperature. A failed one can leave the dryer cold; the high-limit trips on overheat, again usually from a blocked vent.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Gas models — igniter, valve coils, flame sensor</h3>
      <p>On a gas Speed Queen, no-heat usually means the burner isn't lighting: a weak igniter, failed gas-valve solenoid coils, or a bad flame sensor. An igniter that glows then goes out without the gas lighting points to the coils. Gas parts are a tech job — never DIY the gas valve.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Clear the vent, reset the breaker.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Clean the lint filter and the full vent.</strong> Wash the filter, clear the hose behind the dryer, and confirm the exterior flap opens. This fixes most no-heat calls — and removes the fire hazard.</li>
      <li><strong>Reset both breaker legs (electric).</strong> Flip the 240V double breaker fully off and on. A single tripped leg tumbles without heat.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm gas supply (gas).</strong> Verify the gas is on and other gas appliances work. Don't touch the gas valve or line.</li>
      <li><strong>Power-cycle if needed.</strong> Unplug for a minute after cleaning the vent, then restart.</li>
      <li><strong>Watch whether it returns.</strong> Heat restored after a vent cleaning = it was airflow. Still cold on a clear vent = a failed part.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> vent cleaning and the breaker check are safe and fix most Speed Queen no-heat calls. Do not open the cabinet to test the heating element, thermal limit, or fuse, and never disturb the gas line or valve on a gas dryer. A no-heat that survives a full vent cleaning is the handoff point.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When it's a tech job</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Clean vent, still cold? Time for a diagnostic.</h2>

    <p>When the vent is verified clear and the Speed Queen still won't heat, the fault is a single hard part: a burned-out heating element, a tripped or failed thermal limit and fuse (plus the vent that tripped them), or on gas the igniter, valve coils, or flame sensor. Our techs verify the vent first, then test the heating circuit and replace the failed part. Given how long Speed Queens run, a no-heat repair is almost always the easy call over replacing the machine. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/dryer-repair/not-heating/">dryer not heating</a> guide (all brands) and the <a href="/services/dryer-repair/error-codes/">dryer error codes</a> reference. If your Speed Queen takes multiple cycles rather than none, that's <a href="/services/dryer-repair/takes-too-long/">dryer takes too long</a> — usually the same vent. A chronically clogged vent deserves a dedicated <a href="/services/dryer-vent-repair/">dryer vent</a> cleaning. Brand coverage: <a href="/brands/speed-queen/">Speed Queen appliance repair</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/dryer-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/pasadena/dryer-repair/">Pasadena</a>, and <a href="/thousand-oaks/dryer-repair/">Thousand Oaks</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Speed Queen dryer not heating: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Speed Queen dryer still cold after cleaning the vent? Call today.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

